1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,355
What does an ordinary day look like when your home

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:04,200
is circling Earth at

3
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:06,680
28,000 kilometers per hour?

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,467
It begins in a crew cabin about the size of a

5
00:00:09,487 --> 00:00:10,410
phone booth.

6
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:13,220
JEANETTE EPPS: So we sleep in a sleeping bag and we

7
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:14,780
float in our sleeping bag,

8
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,400
and just to keep us in one place,

9
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:19,100
otherwise we would float off everywhere.

10
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,523
The station runs on Greenwich Mean Time,

11
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,910
and wake-up usually comes around six.

12
00:00:24,188 --> 00:00:26,798
For some, the first stop is the window.

13
00:00:27,108 --> 00:00:28,608
MARK VANDE HEI: Every morning I get up,

14
00:00:28,628 --> 00:00:30,128
have a cup of coffee and some snack,

15
00:00:30,148 --> 00:00:32,308
and then I go over to the Cupola,

16
00:00:32,708 --> 00:00:35,148
and I spend 20 minutes meditating lately with

17
00:00:35,168 --> 00:00:36,688
my eyes open and just watching.

18
00:00:36,968 --> 00:00:39,053
Then comes the part people always ask about.

19
00:00:39,403 --> 00:00:41,763
TOM MARSHBURN: The way we stay clean is we use wet

20
00:00:41,783 --> 00:00:42,243
wipes.

21
00:00:42,463 --> 00:00:44,223
We don't have running water up here.

22
00:00:44,243 --> 00:00:46,463
We can't take showers or baths like you might think,

23
00:00:46,483 --> 00:00:48,023
but it's actually pretty nice.

24
00:00:48,283 --> 00:00:50,505
Hair gets washed with no-rinse shampoo.

25
00:00:50,770 --> 00:00:52,380
Breakfast comes in pouches:

26
00:00:52,695 --> 00:00:55,155
add hot water, knead, and heat.

27
00:00:55,395 --> 00:00:57,913
Even salt is a liquid, so loose grains

28
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:00,480
can't drift into vents or eyes.

29
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:02,323
Then the workday begins:

30
00:01:02,638 --> 00:01:04,510
a planning call with mission control,

31
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:06,585
then experiments and repairs.

32
00:01:06,805 --> 00:01:08,927
And no matter how busy the day gets,

33
00:01:09,398 --> 00:01:10,958
STEPHEN BOWEN: You also have to work out,

34
00:01:11,238 --> 00:01:13,698
do a load-bearing exercise for two hours every day to

35
00:01:13,718 --> 00:01:16,257
ensure that you maintain the bone density

36
00:01:16,277 --> 00:01:17,318
and muscle mass.

37
00:01:17,617 --> 00:01:20,152
That means a treadmill with a bungee harness,

38
00:01:20,392 --> 00:01:22,885
a bike with no seat, and a weight machine

39
00:01:22,905 --> 00:01:24,665
that uses vacuum cylinders.

40
00:01:24,892 --> 00:01:27,172
Without it, bones can lose up to one and a

41
00:01:27,192 --> 00:01:28,890
half percent of their mass a month.

42
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:31,295
Evenings and weekends are more personal.

43
00:01:31,725 --> 00:01:34,145
NICHOLE AYERS: We've got satellites that we use to get the

44
00:01:34,165 --> 00:01:36,105
connection with them, so we can call our family,

45
00:01:36,125 --> 00:01:37,205
and actually that's who I call.

46
00:01:37,225 --> 00:01:39,565
I like to call my family and friends on the weekends when we

47
00:01:39,585 --> 00:01:40,645
get some free time.

48
00:01:40,925 --> 00:01:43,635
Crews share dinners, watch movies,

49
00:01:43,713 --> 00:01:45,410
and photograph the planet below.

50
00:01:45,630 --> 00:01:48,340
By nine-thirty, it's back into the sleeping bag,

51
00:01:48,605 --> 00:01:50,715
with tomorrow's schedule already waiting.

52
00:01:50,935 --> 00:01:53,120
Even 400 kilometers above Earth,

53
00:01:53,422 --> 00:01:55,745
life still runs on a routine.

54
00:01:55,985 --> 00:01:57,232
It's just a very different one.
